    "result_text": "All 400/409/429 rejection responses flow through the escalation handlers, and all now emit RFC7807 bodies. \n\n## Summary\n\nAll rejection responses (400, 409, 429) now return an RFC7807 `application/problem+json` body with `type`, `title`, `status`, and `detail`.\n\nThe codebase already had a shared `ProblemDetails.build(...)` helper (used by the 400 validation, 403, 404, and 500 handlers). Nine handlers, though, still returned ad-hoc `{\"error\": ...}` / `{\"errors\": [...]}` JSON. I converted each to the existing pattern \u2014 returning `ResponseEntity<ProblemDetail>` built via the shared helper \u2014 rather than duplicating any logic:\n\n- **400**: `DisposableEmail`, `FutureRegistrationDate`, `InvalidEmail`, `InvalidPostcode`, `InvalidTelephone`, `MissingOwnerFields`\n- **409**: `CityCapacityExceeded`, `DuplicateIdentity`\n- **429**: `DailyLimitExceeded`\n\nEach uses the exception's existing message as `detail`; `type`/`title`/`status` come from the helper. HTTP status codes are unchanged. `MissingOwnerFieldsExceptionHandler` additionally preserves its offending-field list as a `fields` extension member so no diagnostic detail was lost. The now-unused `Error`/`Errors` record types were removed with their handlers (no other code referenced them).\n\nThe framework auto-negotiates `application/problem+json` whenever the body is a `ProblemDetail` (verified against the acceptance test). The full suite is green.",
